  <!-- MISTAKES -->
  <section id="mistakes">
    <div class="eyebrow">Common Errors</div>
    <h2>5 Layering Mistakes You're<br/><em>Probably Making</em></h2>
    <div class="mistakes">
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="mistake-top"><span class="icon">❌</span><span class="m-text">Applying moisturizer before serum</span></div>
        <div class="mistake-fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> Serum always goes before moisturizer. Moisturizer is an occlusive — it creates a physical barrier. Applying serum after it means the serum sits on top of that barrier and never reaches your skin cells.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="mistake-top"><span class="icon">❌</span><span class="m-text">Putting SPF before moisturizer</span></div>
        <div class="mistake-fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> Moisturizer first, wait 2 minutes, then SPF. SPF must be the outermost layer to function correctly. Anything applied over SPF disrupts the UV filter matrix and reduces protection from SPF 50 to potentially SPF 15 or lower.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="mistake-top"><span class="icon">❌</span><span class="m-text">Using Retinol in the morning</span></div>
        <div class="mistake-fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> Retinol is PM-only. UV exposure breaks down Retinol rapidly making it ineffective, and Retinol increases your skin's photosensitivity meaning you'll burn faster. Always night-time, always followed by SPF the next morning.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="mistake-top"><span class="icon">❌</span><span class="m-text">Mixing Vitamin C and Retinol in the same routine</span></div>
        <div class="mistake-fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> Vitamin C is AM. Retinol is PM. Together they cancel each other out and cause irritation. Keep them on opposite sides of the clock and you get maximum benefit from both.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="mistake-top"><span class="icon">❌</span><span class="m-text">Skipping the wait times between steps</span></div>
        <div class="mistake-fix"><strong>Fix:</strong> Wait times exist because actives need time to absorb and reach the correct pH before the next product changes the skin environment. Minimum: 30 seconds between most steps, 60 seconds after Vitamin C, 20-30 minutes after Retinol.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

